What:           /sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/fsin_enable
Date:           September 2026
KernelVersion:  5.10 (TideSense vendor kernel)
Contact:        TideSense maintainers
Description:
                SC7U22-specific camera trigger enable (0/1, default 0).
                Requires non-sleeping fsin-gpios and silan,fsin-pinmux DT
                register/mask/value. Every tenth delivered 200 Hz INT1 IRQ
                raises FSIN; a hard hrtimer lowers it after 100 us.
                Enabling maintains IMU sampling independently of the IIO
                buffer. Disabling drives FSIN low and stops the IMU only
                if no IIO buffer is enabled. IIO buffer transitions do not
                reset an active trigger divider. No PWM fallback.

What:           /sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/fsin_pulse_limit
Date:           September 2026
KernelVersion:  5.10 (TideSense vendor kernel)
Contact:        TideSense maintainers
Description:
                0 for continuous triggers (default), 1..10000000 for a
                finite train. Writable only when fsin_enable is zero.
                Reaching the limit suppresses further pulses but leaves
                fsin_enable set and IMU sampling active until disabled.

What:           /sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/fsin_diagnostics
Date:           September 2026
KernelVersion:  5.10 (TideSense vendor kernel)
Contact:        TideSense maintainers
Description:
                Read-only newline-separated key=value software counters.
                Reports availability, enable, divider, target high width,
                pulse limit/count, last trigger IRQ count, CLOCK_MONOTONIC
                IRQ/GPIO-write timestamps (ns), maximum IRQ-entry-to-write
                and high duration (ns), and skipped triggers caused by a
                still-high output (overruns). Trigger statistics reset on
                each disabled-to-enabled transition, unlike diagnostics'
                cumulative IMU IRQ count. Timestamps do not measure physical
                IRQ-edge latency, camera exposure or IMU filter delay.
